# {{packageName}}

Generate with `redbox-dev-tools init {{hookShortName}} --template v6` in an empty
directory. Install the deployment's qualified v6 core and Fastify **5.12.8**, then
run `npm run build`. The scaffold uses strict TypeScript and public package
entrypoints. Exact pins identify the required artifacts; historical npm packages
with the same core version may lack these exports. For local development use
`npm install --save-dev --save-exact /absolute/path/to/redbox-portal/packages/redbox-core`.

`registerRedboxExtension()` returns **API2** declarations without starting work.
`exampleModule` receives `ExampleInput` (an application-owned writable `auditPath`
and the finalized `config.custom[{{json hookShortName}}].greeting`). Bind the selected
module using `bindExtensionFactory` in `createPortalFastifyApplication().bindings.module`.
Its initialization opens the audit file, activation admits writes, drain awaits
accepted writes, and close releases the handle. Bind migration/bootstrap functions
with `ExampleWorkInput.ensureNamedQuery`, the deployment's actual repository upsert.

Supply `http.bindings` to `createPortalFastifyApplication`, returning
`bindControllerFactory(exampleController, { example })` and the bindings returned
by `bindGeneratedControllers`. Use a lazy borrowed operation if the module has not
yet been constructed. Pass the resolved application to `createPortalFastifyHost`;
the production host owns authentication, policies, CSRF, errors, the listener and
signals. Set the canonical application URL to the actual published origin.

The GET `/hooks/{{hookShortName}}/status` action explicitly permits public access.
POST `/:branding/:portal/hooks/{{hookShortName}}/greet` selects the ordered
production authentication/brand/role policies and CSRF. Its role rule permits
`Admin`; browser callers supply a session token and canonical Origin. Public
access does not bypass CSRF on mutations. Constructors acquire no resources;
Fastify actions borrow operations and serialize JSON before submitting the reply.
Typed errors pass to the host's safe error boundary without a generic catch.

Add a controller from this directory:

```sh
redbox-dev-tools --root . generate controller Catalogue --routes 'read:get:/:branding/:portal/catalogue:Admin'
redbox-dev-tools --root . generate method --file src/controllers/CatalogueController.ts --method publish --http POST --route '/:branding/:portal/catalogue/publish' --auth Admin
```

Generation updates `config/extensionControllers.ts` and `config/extensionRoutes.ts`,
already included in API2 registration, plus the explicit action allowlist/input
interface for new methods. Supply each named operation in the generated typed
`GeneratedControllerInputs`; requests remain live after `beforeAction` hooks.
Use `--public` to select public access explicitly; the default is protected.
`--auth` adds role rules when `auth.config.ts` is present. Programmatic callers
can supply per-route `access` with public, named policies or deny. Browser and
webservice controller identities remain distinct. Webservice routes retain executable
API metadata, so verified bearer mutations use the host's API CSRF adapter;
cookie sessions still require CSRF and all mutations retain Origin checks.
For `--webservice --public`, merge the exported `apiPolicies` into the application's
explicit `http.policyConfiguration` after its base mappings. API policies precede
that mapping: the generated per-action `true` entry permits only the selected
public actions; other actions retain their configured protection.
Reruns with different route target/access/CSRF/API metadata fail explicitly.
Adding a method to `src/controllers/ExampleController.ts` preserves its existing
`exampleController` registration; also supply the new operation when binding it.

`generate angular-app review-app --ejs-view review-view --auth Admin` emits a
registered page controller with a valid action identifier. Bind its typed
`render(request, reply, view, title)` operation to the application's shared page
renderer (`prepareNativePage`, `renderPageShell`, `sendPageResponse`) and session
CSRF operation. It passes the generated EJS view/title explicitly; it needs no globals. Non-HTTP modules, work,
configuration, package-relative views and assets retain their lifecycle owners.

The source-linked production qualification is
`packages/redbox-core/test/http/fastify/GeneratedExtensionMongo.test.ts` in the
portal. Clean packed-consumer qualification belongs to FM-039. A generated
extension does not start a separate portal process or expose raw Fastify registration.
